{"name":"Death of a Star","hex":"#e760d2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"death","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The cessation of life and all associated processes; the end of an organism's existence as an entity independent from its environment and its return to an inert, non-living state.","Execution (in the judicial sense)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/death"},{"term":"star","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any small, natural and bright dot in the sky, most visible in the night or twilight sky. This sense includes the planets, but it is now sometimes used in exclusion of them due to influence from the technical astronomical sense.","A planet thought to influence one's fate; (figuratively) fate or luck."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To appear as a featured performer or headliner, especially in an entertainment program.","To feature (a performer or a headliner), especially in a movie or an entertainment program."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/star"}],"entries":[{"title":"Death","blocks":[{"text":"Death is the end of an organism's life. It is the irreversible cessation of biological functions that sustain a living organism. Entropy, nature's tendency toward disorder, can contribute to the breakdown of living systems over time. Upon death, organisms stop exhibiting behavior and generally begin to decompose through various physical and chemical processes. Death can occur at any stage of development, including in infancy. A person may experience clinical death that is reversible, resulting in some cases in a near-death experience. In humans, establishing death of the brain determines that an individual has irreversibly died."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Hendrick_Andriessen_-_Vanity_Piece_-_1914-DE_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Ghent_%28MSK%29.jpg/330px-Hendrick_Andriessen_-_Vanity_Piece_-_1914-DE_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Ghent_%28MSK%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death","wikidata":"Q4"},{"title":"Star","blocks":[{"text":"A star is a luminous spheroid of plasma held together by self-gravity. The nearest star to Earth is the Sun. Many other stars are visible to the naked eye at night; their immense distances from Earth make them appear as fixed points of light. The most prominent stars have been categorised into constellations and asterisms, and many of the brightest stars have proper names. Astronomers have assembled star catalogues that identify the known stars and provide standardized stellar designations. The observable universe contains an estimated 1022 to 1024 stars. Only about 4,000 of these stars are visible to the naked eye—all within the Milky Way galaxy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg/330px-The_Sun_in_white_light.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Star","wikidata":"Q523"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/French_-_Pendant_with_a_Monk_and_Death_-_Walters_71461.jpg/500px-French_-_Pendant_with_a_Monk_and_Death_-_Walters_71461.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/French_-_Pendant_with_a_Monk_and_Death_-_Walters_71461.jpg/1280px-French_-_Pendant_with_a_Monk_and_Death_-_Walters_71461.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/French_-_Pendant_with_a_Monk_and_Death_-_Walters_71461.jpg/500px-French_-_Pendant_with_a_Monk_and_Death_-_Walters_71461.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"French 16th-/17th-century ivory pendant, Monk and Death, recalling mortality and the certainty of death (Walters Art Museum)[30]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrench_-_Pendant_with_a_Monk_and_Death_-_Walters_71461.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Postmortem_interval_changes_%28stages_of_death%29.png/500px-Postmortem_interval_changes_%28stages_of_death%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Postmortem_interval_changes_%28stages_of_death%29.png/1280px-Postmortem_interval_changes_%28stages_of_death%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Postmortem_interval_changes_%28stages_of_death%29.png/500px-Postmortem_interval_changes_%28stages_of_death%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Timeline of postmortem changes (stages of death)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APostmortem_interval_changes_(stages_of_death).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Lewis_Hine%2C_Newsies_smoking_at_Skeeter%27s_Branch%2C_St._Louis%2C_1910.jpg/500px-Lewis_Hine%2C_Newsies_smoking_at_Skeeter%27s_Branch%2C_St._Louis%2C_1910.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Lewis_Hine%2C_Newsies_smoking_at_Skeeter%27s_Branch%2C_St._Louis%2C_1910.jpg/1280px-Lewis_Hine%2C_Newsies_smoking_at_Skeeter%27s_Branch%2C_St._Louis%2C_1910.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Lewis_Hine%2C_Newsies_smoking_at_Skeeter%27s_Branch%2C_St._Louis%2C_1910.jpg/500px-Lewis_Hine%2C_Newsies_smoking_at_Skeeter%27s_Branch%2C_St._Louis%2C_1910.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"American children smoking in 1910. Tobacco smoking caused an estimated 100 million dea